    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d170">

                <pb facs="0168" n="158" sameAs="frus:frus1937v05/pg158"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.34 Leasing/28: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Scotten</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-08-13T19:00:00-03:00">August 13, 1937—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:19 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>93. From Ambassador Caffery.<anchor n="13" xml:id="d170-f1"/> During my first
                    call this afternoon upon the Minister of Foreign Affairs the latter, who had
                    just returned from a conference with President Vargas, showed me a telegram from
                        Aranha<anchor n="14" xml:id="d170-f2"/> regarding Ambassador Espil’s<anchor
                        n="15" xml:id="d170-f3"/> visit to the Department to set out his
                    Government’s point of view in regard to postponing the destroyer matter until
                    the holding of a naval conference. Aranha recommends that, should the American
                    Government agree with the point of view of the Argentine, Brazil should
                    relinquish the project of obtaining the destroyers but should at the same time
                    make it clear that Brazil cannot in any way admit the right of a third power to
                    interfere in this question. The Minister then declared that he would at once
                    telephone Aranha that the President and he agreed with him but he added
                    significantly “we do not believe that the American Government will agree with
                    the stand taken by Argentina”. [Caffery.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Scotten</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d170-f1" n="13" xml:id="d170-f1-content">
                <p>Jefferson Caffery, appointed Ambassador to Brazil; Mr. Caffery presented his
                    letters of credence on August 17.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d170-f2" n="14" xml:id="d170-f2-content">
                <p>Oswaldo Aranha, Brazilian Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d170-f3" n="15" xml:id="d170-f3-content">
                <p>Felipe A. Espil, Argentine Ambassador in the United States.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
